<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/19th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · War</p><h1 class="enc-title">Cochinchina campaign</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1858">1858</a>–<a href="/years/1862">1862</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · <a href="/encyclopedia/wars">All wars</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Cochinchina campaign was a war fought from 1858 to 1862 by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> against the Nguyễn dynasty of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vietnam">Vietnam</a>. Begun as a punitive expedition over the persecution of Catholic missionaries, it ended with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-saigon-1862">Treaty of Saigon (1862)</a>, by which Vietnam ceded three southern provinces, the beginning of French Cochinchina and of French rule in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indochina">Indochina</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/cochinchina-campaign.jpg" alt="Cochinchina campaign" width="320" height="183" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Antoine Léon Morel-Fatio · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Prise_de_Saigon_18_Fevrier_1859_Antoine_Morel-Fatio.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>French missionaries had worked in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vietnam">Vietnam</a> since the 17th century, and by the mid-19th century Bishop Pellerin counted 600,000 Catholic converts in Annam and Tonkin, led mostly by French and Spanish clergy whom many Vietnamese distrusted. The persecution of missionaries under the emperors Minh Mạng and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thieu-tri">Thiệu Trị</a> in the 1840s drew only sporadic French responses. In 1857, however, the execution of two Spanish missionaries approved by Emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tu-duc">Tự Đức</a> coincided with a French expedition to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-opium-war">Second Opium War</a>, and in November Napoleon III authorised Admiral <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-rigault-de-genouilly">Charles Rigault de Genouilly</a> to lead a punitive expedition.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>In September 1858 a French and Spanish force captured <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/da-nang">Tourane</a> (Da Nang), but the Vietnamese Christians did not rise as Pellerin had predicted, and an army under Nguyễn Tri Phương pinned the allies down in a siege of nearly three years in which disease caused heavy losses. Rigault de Genouilly turned south and took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ho-chi-minh-city">Saigon</a> on 17 February 1859, demolishing its citadel and burning its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rice">rice</a> granaries on 8 March. A garrison of about 1,000 men was then besieged in Saigon from March 1860 to February 1861, while Tourane was evacuated.</p><p>With the war in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> over, Admiral Léonard Charner brought 70 ships and 3,500 soldiers to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ho-chi-minh-city">Saigon</a> and broke the siege lines at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-ky-hoa">Battle of Ky Hoa</a> on 24 and 25 February 1861. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/my-tho">Mỹ Tho</a> fell on 12 April without a shot. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tu-duc">Tự Đức</a> turned to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guerrilla-warfare">guerrilla</a> warfare, and French units roving the countryside fanned resistance by their brutality towards suspected insurgents. Admiral Louis Adolphe Bonard took Biên Hòa on 16 December 1861 and Vĩnh Long on 23 March 1862, after which Tự Đức sued for peace.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tu-duc">Tự Đức</a>'s minister Phan Thanh Giản signed the treaty with Bonard and the Spanish representative, Colonel Carlos Palanca Gutiérrez, on 5 June 1862. Besides the provinces of Biên Hòa, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gia-dinh">Gia Định</a> and Định Tường, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vietnam">Vietnam</a> ceded the islands of Poulo Condore, legalised Catholic worship, opened the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mekong-river">Mekong</a> and three ports to French trade and agreed to pay one million dollars over ten years.</p><p>In 1867 Admiral Pierre de la Grandière forced the cession of Châu Đốc, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ha-tien">Hà Tiên</a> and Vĩnh Long, which <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tu-duc">Tự Đức</a> recognised only in the 1874 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-saigon-1874">Treaty of Saigon</a>. Spain, the junior partner, gained a share of the indemnity but no territory and withdrew its last troops in 1863. Peace also freed Tự Đức to crush a rising in Tonkin led by the Catholic nobleman Le Bao Phung, who was captured, tortured and put to death.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Cochinchina campaign” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
